## 1. Critique of v1 (what was wrong and the fix)

### Critical (would break or endanger users)

| # | Problem in v1 | Fix in v2 |
|---|---|---|
| C1 | **SSRF guard is a hostname blocklist.** It misses DNS rebinding, IPv6 (`::1`, `fc00::/7`, `::ffff:127.0.0.1`), decimal/octal/hex IPs, and redirects to private IPs. | Resolve DNS server-side, validate **every resolved IP** against a deny CIDR list (v4+v6), connect to the validated IP, **re-validate each redirect hop (max 3)**, port allowlist (80/443/8080/8443), 10 s timeout, 2 MB response cap, strip `cookie`/`authorization` unless the user set them explicitly, per-user rate limit. See §6.2. |
| C2 | **Share-by-email can be hijacked.** `shared_with_email` matched against an unverified email lets an attacker register that address and read the item. | Only match on **verified** email (`email_confirmed_at is not null`). Pending shares are claimed by a DB function on first verified sign-in. Invites carry a hashed, expiring token. |
| C3 | **Authorization is hand-written SQL predicates in Express.** One missed route leaks another tenant's data. | **Postgres Row-Level Security** is the source of truth. The client talks to Supabase directly; Edge Functions only run as the user's JWT. No service-role key in any code path that handles user input. See §5. |
| C4 | **"Zero-knowledge" `AES-256-GCM` is under-specified** and conflicts with server features (search, annotations, MCP read). | Defined model in §6.3: encrypted items are opaque to the server and to MCP, cannot be shared except by sharing the passphrase out of band, **no recovery**, with the UI warning before first use. |
| C5 | **Secret scrubbing is presented as a guarantee** (`[VERIFIED 99%+]`). Regex scrubbing has false negatives. | Label it **best-effort**, always show a redaction diff preview before copy/send, use gitleaks/secretlint-style rule sets plus entropy checks, ship a labeled test corpus and track recall. Never claim "safe". |
| C6 | **Rust `regex` semantics ≠ JavaScript.** The Rust crate has no backreferences or lookaround. App 3 users debug regexes they will run in JS or Python. | The regex explorer uses native `RegExp` (JS flavor) by default. A "Rust flavor" tab uses Wasm. Python flavor is a later option. Never claim a JS regex works because the Rust one does. |

### Major (design gaps)

| # | Problem | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| M1 | **Not free.** Cloud SQL + a hosted Express server cost money and run always-on. | D2. |
| M2 | **Owner stored twice** (`organizations.ownerId` and `members.role=owner`). They drift; there is no last-owner or ownership-transfer rule. | Single source: `organization_members.role`. A trigger blocks removing or demoting the last owner. Add an explicit transfer-ownership action. |
| M3 | **No version history table**, but `version int` and "visual diffing" are promised. | Add `vault_item_versions`; optimistic locking via `version` on update. |
| M4 | **Pending membership/invite state missing.** `invitedEmail` lives on the members row, so you can't represent "invited but not joined". | Separate `organization_invites` table. |
| M5 | **MCP Hub is hand-waved.** `/api/mcp/execute` is undefined; MCP clients need a real MCP server (stdio or Streamable HTTP) and tokens. "Auto-suggest router" implies an LLM nobody pays for. | §9: a remote MCP server (Streamable HTTP) on an Edge Function, per-user scoped hashed tokens, a small tool set, keyword/BM25 router (no LLM) in v1. |
| M6 | **`mcp_routing_logs.taskPrompt` stores user prompts.** Privacy and retention liability. | Store hash and length by default; full text is opt-in with a 30-day TTL. |
| M7 | **Token counting.** "Exact" counts differ per model. | Show "≈ tokens" with a selectable tokenizer (o200k, cl100k, Claude estimate). Label estimates as estimates. |
| M8 | **API Runner chaining `{{step1.token}}` is undefined** (language, scoping, safety). | §7.2: JSONPath extraction only, no `eval`, masked secrets, cycle detection. |
| M9 | **Guest→cloud migration** has no idempotency or conflict rules, and localStorage is the wrong store (5 MB). | IndexedDB (via `idb-keyval`), each local item gets a client UUID; import uses `upsert on conflict (id)` so a retry is safe; size quota enforced. |
| M10 | **`/?docs=<topicId>`** hijacks the home route and breaks SEO and back-button behavior. | `/docs/:topicId` (with `?docs=` redirecting to it). |
| M11 | **`(i)` hover popovers** fail keyboard and touch users. | Click/Enter/focus toggle, `aria-describedby`, Esc closes, no hover-only content. |
| M12 | **Missing entirely**: testing, CI, observability, rate limits, data export, account deletion, backups, perf budgets, a11y audit, license attributions, browser support. | §10 and §11. |

### Minor / wording

- "Warm stone `#F8FAFC`" is actually a cool slate-50. Fix the name or the value.
- The audit table says "ADOPTED" for repos that are really **inspiration** (plannotator, claude-mem, lobehub, zed, Infisical). Don't imply code reuse or license compatibility. Re-labeled in §12.
- Remove `[VERIFIED 99%+]` (unfalsifiable). `morluto/rea` was rejected, but I could not confirm that repo exists; keep it as "not evaluated".
- Don't hand-write what libraries already do: cron explanation (`cronstrue`), cron parsing (`cron-parser`), regex AST (`regexp-tree`), JSONPath (`jsonpath-plus`), zip (`fflate`), diff (`diff` / `jsdiff`), split panes (`react-resizable-panels`), command palette (`cmdk`), popovers/dialogs (Radix UI).
- Top nav is "4 destinations" but then lists org switcher, settings, theme, ⌘K, docs. State it as **4 destinations + 4 utilities** so the rule is testable.

