## 16. Billing: when, why, how
- **Under the $0 rule (D10) billing is deferred indefinitely.** Everything below is a *future option*, not a deliverable.
- **Tier A and B: no billing.** Teams are invited manually; everything is free. Building billing early is pure cost.
- **Tier C trigger (add billing only when ONE of these is true):** Supabase DB > 350 MB (70% of the free 500 MB), monthly Edge Function invocations > 300k (of 500k), more than ~50 active orgs, or you want revenue. Until then stay free.
- **Proposed plans (decide later, nothing built now):**

| | Free | Team (per org, monthly) |
|---|---|---|
| Personal vault | 20 MB | 20 MB per member |
| Org vault | 1 org, ≤ 5 members, 100 MB | ≤ 50 members, 2 GB (needs Supabase Storage/Pro) |
| MCP tokens | 2 | 20 |
| Proxy runs | 200/day | 5,000/day |
| Audit-log retention | 30 days | 1 year |

- **Tooling:** use a merchant of record (Lemon Squeezy or Paddle) so VAT/sales tax is handled for you; Stripe is cheaper but you handle tax. A `subscriptions` table plus a webhook Edge Function (signature verified, idempotent by event id) sets `orgs.plan`; limits are enforced in RLS/RPC, not just the UI. Only you can create the payment account and enter any financial details; I can build the code around it.
- **Prerequisites:** ToS, privacy policy, refund policy, invoices, and a downgrade path (over-quota orgs become read-only, never deleted).

## 17. Vault size and quotas
- **512 KB per item** (stored JSON, Postgres compresses large values). Large packs, such as whole-repo packs, **stay local or export to a file**; the UI explains this when you hit the limit.
- **20 MB per user, 100 MB per org** on the free tier; enforced by an insert/update trigger summing `pg_column_size(content)`. Show a usage bar in Settings.
- Version history keeps the **last 20 versions per item** (older ones pruned) so history can't silently eat the quota.
- Later (paid): attachments in Supabase Storage (1 GB free) instead of JSONB.
- Why these numbers: 500 MB free DB ÷ 20 MB ≈ 25 full-quota users, so the free tier supports a small team comfortably; most real items (a template, a log filter, an API chain) are under 20 KB.

## 18. Auth setup: Google + GitHub + email
1. Supabase → Auth → Providers: enable Google and GitHub; set the site URL and redirect URLs (local `http://localhost:5173`, preview, production).
2. Google Cloud Console → OAuth consent screen (External; app name, support email, authorized domain) → OAuth client (Web) with Supabase's callback URL. Scopes: `openid email profile` only. Publish the app to leave "Testing" mode (needed before strangers can sign in).
3. GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → OAuth Apps → callback = Supabase's; scope `user:email` only.
4. **Account linking:** the same verified email via Google and GitHub must resolve to **one** profile. Enable Supabase's automatic linking only for verified emails; a GitHub account with an unverified primary email must not link or claim shares. Test this (e2e): Google first then GitHub, and the reverse.
5. Email/password: confirmation required, password min 12, breached-password check on, rate limit on login/reset.
6. Handle (`@name`) is chosen on first login (suggest one from the provider name; check uniqueness).

## 19. Glossary (plain English)
- **Proxy:** a small server function that sends the API request for App 2 on the user's behalf. Browsers block most cross-site requests (CORS), so without it many APIs can't be tested. It's risky because attackers could aim it at internal addresses (SSRF), which is why §6.2 is strict.
- **"Ships last / ships" means "is released/delivered"**: the proxy is built in Phase 6, after the security basics (RLS, tokens) are proven, because it is the riskiest piece.
- **RLS:** database rules that decide, per row, who can read or change it.
- **SSRF:** tricking a server into calling addresses it shouldn't (internal network, cloud metadata).
- **MCP:** the protocol AI apps (Claude, Cursor) use to call external tools.
- **Wasm:** compiled Rust running in the browser.
- **Tier A/B/C:** Portfolio / Small team / Public SaaS launch levels (§11).

